{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgec5ugiQyTcSJBx4qtpw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've committed WI-978's sitting 013 as 165e0b1f8 (`docs/reviews/wi-978/013-ADJUDICATE-d3cfb73.md`). I'd bless both amended rows, approve LLR-363 and TC-402, and return LLR-362 and TC-401. The commit ends with `WI: WI-978` and `Loop-Session: 20261102T035202Z-8dc8b4` in the same trailer block.\n\n**Amendment** (`VERDICT: MEANING rows=2`):\n- **SR-174 (MEANING, bless):** it widens identity allocation from work items to the registry ids that parallel lanes author, drawn from one shared reservation record, and adds three acceptance clauses. Keeping the id is argued as PROCESS.md §4 asks: everything that already cites it is listed in the Rationale and still reads the unchanged work-item half.\n  - **The sentence form:** the Requirement now runs to several sentences, which made me check the one-`shall` rule. I read the extra sentences as where each kind of id is minted, serving the single \"allocated at most once\" obligation, not as separate obligations. The acceptance also needs the reservation concept they introduce. The text is the spec's own target wording.\n- **TC-158 (MEANING, bless):** its old text is kept verbatim. The added checks are that a mark rise covered by a reservation record passes, a rise above every record fails, and an altered or deleted committed record fails. Each has a test that exists and passes.\n\n**First approval** (`OUTCOME: RETURN rows=4`):\n- **LLR-363 and TC-402 (APPROVE):** the landing rules are complete for both landing routes, including sitting 008's refusal of a re-authored retired id. TC-402's 25 tests cover every case.\n- **LLR-362 (RETURN):** it's meant to be the allocation row, but it never states the allocation rule. The code starts each range one above the larger of the carrier's mark and trunk's committed mark, which is what makes ranges disjoint. Also missing:\n  - that a lost race or a refused reserve writes nothing;\n  - that the first use creates the carrier from trunk's committed watermark;\n  - what a fold produces;\n  - SR-174's \"a mark rises only by allocation, correction or reservation\", whose code is traced to this very row.\n\n  It also never defines \"the carrier\".\n- **TC-401 (RETURN):** it goes back with its parent. Its expected results also leave out the first-use seeding that one of its own tests drives.\n\n**Checks:** `trace.py --strict` exits 0. The carrier and watermark unit suites pass (112 tests).\n\n**The act:** the rung is released, so the act turn can re-attest SR-174 and TC-158 and flip LLR-363 and TC-402. LLR-362 and TC-401 stay Drafted and re-sit together. The fixes are drafted under a `### Dispositions` heading inside `## first-approval`, as returns the lane answers before they re-sit.\n\nThe commit hooks passed, but again reported that the `format` check didn't run (ruff isn't installed). I committed only the verdict, so `013-ADJUDICATE-d3cfb73.md.requested` is still untracked."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":691,"cache_read_input_tokens":536325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":691},"output_tokens":749,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"5d5cb27c-6a88-4069-9a0d-dae61f41dffe","timestamp":"2026-11-02T18:52:08.194Z","request_id":"req_011Cgec5uSKzRRGNHqKYf2xB"}
